WEBVTT

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:35.960
While Phoebe stays behind with her nanny, the recovery team run deep into the bush.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:49.440
South Africa, and it's the start of another season for the Ramberts.

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:51.800
What noise does a lion make?

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:52.280
Rragh!

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:53.880
Shaun has been catching big game for over 18 years.

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:55.600
Close that gate!

00:00:55.240 --> 00:01:01.080
His English wife, Emma, has been a vet in Africa for six years.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:02.600
We don't need Daddy!

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:05.040
You drive, I'll sit here!

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:07.520
Going everywhere with them is their daughter Phoebe...

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:08.560
Who's in the helicopter?

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:10.760
Daddy.

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:15.440
..And their dog, Taula.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:17.160
The little mutt earned her keep today.

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:21.360
Flying for them again is Big John.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:26.640
This is just one or two rogue animals that don't listen to the helicopter.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:27.080
They just stand there and look at you.

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:31.240
Following in the family footsteps this year is his son, Little John.

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:36.440
At just 20 years old, he's the youngest game capture pilot in South Africa.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:37.200
He did fantastically well.

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:38.600
Very happy with the way he's flying.

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:43.400
Backing them up is a 20-strong capture team.

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:49.520
And then, there's all the wildlife South Africa has to offer.

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:51.840
What do you see?

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:53.280
A camera.

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:54.240
Can you see a camera?!

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:10.960
The first job of the year is at Madikwe, a 75,000 hectare game park.

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:16.760
Once farmland, now it's home to over 15,000 wild animals.

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:24.720
Including the big five: Lions, leopards, rhinos, elephants and buffalo.

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:29.880
All can kill, and it makes capture here particularly dangerous.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:34.840
Emma's first job is to take a look at a cheetah with a wounded leg that has been found on the reserve.

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:36.920
He was about to die.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.520
When he was picked up, he was skin and bones.

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:43.720
If we released him now, he would die, definitely.

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:48.080
He's got to be 100% fit to be able to go back into Madikwe, there's lots of things that will kill him there.

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:52.920
There's lions, they'll take out a cheetah with pleasure.

00:02:52.360 --> 00:03:02.000
We're going to just put a dart into him, and then I'm going to see what the leg is, an old break, a tumour, an abscess, it could be anything.

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:02.560
I can't tell from here.

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:03.320
I'll do 200 then.

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:07.800
We have to dart, and that's a risk in itself when you're darting a sick animal.

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:09.400
I'm probably going to use a lower dose on him.

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:15.640
Madikwe's resident ecologist is Declan Hofmeyr.

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:15.880
Morning.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:17.360
Good morning.

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:21.000
Move into the other corner, I'll just walk down there.

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:22.080
You could probably walk in if you need to.

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:26.080
He's not aggressive, he's just a little nervous.

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:27.120
Let's go in.

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:30.280
I'd prefer to get the shot in rather than be quite far away.

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:31.120
He's just lying down.

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:39.120
Cheetah are sort of renowned as being the softer cat.

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:45.320
They're not usually aggressive, but under stress, in this situation, they've got the best weapons, claws and teeth.

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:49.760
OK, the dart's in.

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:53.280
It's fallen out, so I'm a bit suspicious where it hit.

00:03:52.400 --> 00:04:00.520
It definitely hit, but it might have been under the skin, or hit a bone.

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:03.600
We'll give him a few minutes and make sure the drug is taking effect.

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:15.280
Across the reserve, Shaun and the capture team are getting ready to do some darting.

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:21.320
The plan for today is to catch some buffalo.

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:27.440
Being the first day the herds are intact, they're not aware the helicopters are coming to catch them.

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:30.280
They may look like cows, but they are not to be trifled with.

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:35.640
Buffalo can weigh up to a tonne, and run at 45mph, easily outpacing any sprinter.

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:45.000
Their horns make them lethal, as Shaun nearly found out last year.

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:57.880
Today is a big day for Little John, LJ to his friends.

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:02.160
It's his first capture, but it hasn't got off to a flying start.

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:09.920
The helicopter won't start.

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:14.440
Problem being, I think the battery's a bit flat, a bit old.

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:18.440
Shaun's not gonna be impressed if there's no helicopter in the air.

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:26.200
The temperature in the next hour is going to be 26, 27 degrees.

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:29.920
Buffalo being a dark-skinned animal, more susceptible to heat.

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:33.960
Soon as the temperature gets above 26 degrees, we put a dart in, they run 400-500 metres.

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:34.880
They become too heat-stressed.

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:40.480
ENGINE REFUSES TO START Is it flooding or not?

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:40.920
I've flooded it.

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:42.360
OK, now leave it, leave it, leave it.

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:44.720
Switch it all off, let the charge...

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:48.040
It'll come back in a minute.

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:52.720
LJ's attempts at a jump-start haven't worked, but he knows a man who could help - his dad.

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:54.520
OK.

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:57.600
OK, I'll get back to you, I'll let you know about that.

00:05:57.080 --> 00:05:58.760
Cheers, bye.

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:07.360
We'll crack it with mixture closed, see if at least it gets it out, and then we can see if she catches.

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:08.440
OK.

00:06:07.480 --> 00:06:11.120
I'm gonna have a cup of tea, you go try that.

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:13.360
Give me a shout when you start it.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:23.680
He's gone down quite quickly, and that worries me because he's not the healthiest cheetah and the drug dose might have been a bit too high.

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:29.120
We'll get in and monitor him.

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:42.080
Because he's such a high-risk anaesthetic, I have this fabulous beepy toy called a pulse oximeter which just monitors his pulse rate and his oxygen levels.

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:45.400
It makes very comforting beeping noises.

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:47.200
Phoebe, do you want to come here and see him?

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:48.800
Do you want to touch?

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:49.320
Touch?

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:49.560
Touch!

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:50.560
Touch.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:57.160
He's put on weight, so you can imagine what he was like when Declan picked him up.

00:06:56.320 --> 00:07:00.000
He's still underweight, but some muscle mass there.

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:00.400
This is the bad leg.

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:02.400
His knee is in place.

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:04.880
We've got a very hard, bony swelling here.

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:06.760
Cheetah!

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:08.040
Isn't it, with a sore leg.

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:17.160
PHOEBE BABBLES I'm looking at tumour, or old break that's healing.

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:19.120
I can't tell the difference.

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:19.280
If it started to get bigger...

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:22.120
I would put money on a tumour.

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:24.480
But only time will tell.

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:27.800
If it turns out to be cancerous, he'll have to be put down.

00:07:27.800 --> 00:07:35.640
Our cheetah population has taken a dive over the last few years, in direct correspondence to the increase in lion population.

00:07:35.080 --> 00:07:40.320
Lion compete with everything, and anything gets in their way, they will try and push out of an area.

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:44.960
And in extreme circumstances, if the animal's not fast enough, kill them.

00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:49.960
Which is why we're down to four free-roaming cheetah in the reserve.

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:50.720
Plus this one.

00:07:49.840 --> 00:07:52.800
It'll be nice to get him back out there.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:54.080
I wouldn't want to call it a day on him...

00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:58.120
I'm glad you say that, cos I don't want to either.

00:07:57.160 --> 00:07:58.560
No, he's a bit important.

00:07:57.600 --> 00:07:58.760
Touch!

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:00.360
Would you like to touch?

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:00.720
Cheetah!

00:08:01.960 --> 00:08:03.840
It is a cheetah, you're clever!

00:08:03.080 --> 00:08:05.000
Cheetah.

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:14.440
.. We're gonna leave him now, and he'll stay in the pens probably for another month, getting big and strong, and we'll see whether he can be released back into the wild.

00:08:14.920 --> 00:08:17.960
Emma and Phoebe are off to join the capture team.

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:18.600
ENGINE STARTS THEN STALLS Aaargh!

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:32.320
A bit of fatherly advice pays off, and the capture can finally get under way.

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:41.960
Right LJ, first buffalo?

00:08:41.159 --> 00:08:42.960
First buffalo, Shaun.

00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:45.640
What can I expect?

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:50.800
Well, we have to find them first.

00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:56.640
You concentrate on the flying part, and we'll just guide them, and then try and get them to a road.

00:08:55.720 --> 00:09:01.880
There's got to be a lot of skill on your part to try and get them to drop on the road.

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:10.800
Who's in the helicopter?

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:11.800
Daddy.

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:20.760
The Madikwe buffalo are renowned for their blood line.

00:09:19.960 --> 00:09:27.000
Each one can command a price of up to £15,000.

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:32.120
Where's the buffalo?

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:33.560
Are they there?

00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:34.360
Are they?

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:37.560
OK, there, that female in the middle there.

00:09:42.280 --> 00:09:44.760
OVER 'OK, we've got buffalo here.

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:49.000
'We're just going to come up to where the helicopter is.'

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:51.920
Emma leads the capture team towards the buffalo.

00:09:52.600 --> 00:09:57.560
'Keep straight on the pipeline, do not turn off from the road.

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:03.000
'Straight on the pipeline.'

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:05.240
Hang on, stand by.

00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:09.560
OK, in the middle, to the left.

00:10:12.640 --> 00:10:18.200
Are you showing any sign, yet?

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:21.640
'Um, negative.'

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:28.720
Once the dart is in, they only have minutes to get to the animal before it starts overheating.

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:33.960
The closer it falls to the road, the easier it is to get it on to a lorry.

00:10:33.320 --> 00:10:36.560
We've got a slight problem, we've got a jumbo on the road.

00:10:35.720 --> 00:10:37.080
So, I'm just going to have to take this a bit slow.

00:10:36.440 --> 00:10:40.480
Bull elephants have to be treated very carefully.

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:43.920
If they feel threatened they'll charge the car.

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:48.120
The elephants are really curious and not really afraid of humans.

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:48.600
'The elephant's on your right.'

00:10:51.720 --> 00:10:53.800
Declan did a bit of a barrier thing and then I whizzed by him.

00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:55.760
'OK.'

00:10:58.600 --> 00:11:00.960
I've got a few external factors in Madikwe.

00:11:00.160 --> 00:11:04.680
And one of the biggest worries is lions and elephants in the area.

00:11:04.120 --> 00:11:08.120
But personally, the biggest worry today is that Phoebe's teething.

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:10.560
I think I'm more scared of her teething than any lions!

00:11:14.080 --> 00:11:17.080
'He's right under the helicopter now.'

00:11:17.720 --> 00:11:20.360
Where he is now, run!

00:11:19.600 --> 00:11:24.040
LJ's first buffalo has fallen far from the road.

00:11:23.320 --> 00:11:28.000
While Phoebe stays behind with her nanny, the recovery team run deep into the bush.

00:11:32.040 --> 00:11:38.440
This is lion country and the rest of the b uffalo herd are still nearby.

00:11:38.840 --> 00:11:42.920
We're quite far off the road, this is probably our worst recovery.

00:11:42.640 --> 00:11:47.440
They can't get through to us here.

00:11:46.640 --> 00:11:51.520
The worst thing about landing in a place like this is that you don't feel safe.

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:52.840
It's somebody else's territory.

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:54.400
Let's get him sternal.

00:11:53.800 --> 00:11:54.920
I don't like this position.

00:11:55.040 --> 00:12:04.120
The only way out for this buffalo is by pick-up but the bush is impenetrably thick.

00:12:03.240 --> 00:12:07.400
LJ is still circling above.

00:12:06.920 --> 00:12:10.640
As a pilot, I've got two jobs.

00:12:09.760 --> 00:12:14.360
I've got one to get Shaun to dart the animal.

00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:32.360
And number two, Madikwe being a big game reserve, when Emma's near the buffalo, I've got to look out for lions, and any buffalo that are hiding in the thicket We're in the middle of a deep thicket, and we've got a puncture.

00:12:31.400 --> 00:12:36.160
But as you can see, we don't need a jack as we've got guys lifting the vehicle!

00:12:35.840 --> 00:12:44.480
We've got a herd of buffalo probably about 600 metres, LJ's busy trying to push them from us.

00:12:43.760 --> 00:12:48.080
We can't see until they're about five metres in front of us.

00:12:47.360 --> 00:12:52.320
The herd is running directly towards Emma.

00:12:51.440 --> 00:12:56.480
If LJ doesn't manage to change their course, the team will be trampled.

00:12:55.840 --> 00:13:01.800
With the buffalo bearing down on them the only protection is the pick-up truck.

00:13:00.920 --> 00:13:02.160
'Everyone on the buggy!'

00:13:01.240 --> 00:13:04.440
Quickly, quickly, quickly!

00:13:14.720 --> 00:13:16.440
Everyone on the buggy!

00:13:21.960 --> 00:13:26.000
'OK, they've turned.'

00:13:28.720 --> 00:13:35.840
Right, he says it's safe.

00:13:36.840 --> 00:13:41.160
A slight panic, as the buffalo were coming towards us there.

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:44.840
It's so thick, so there was a mad rush to get to the buggy.

00:13:44.200 --> 00:13:46.760
They're just passed to the side of us.

00:13:45.920 --> 00:13:47.520
But LJ managed to turn them away.

00:13:49.760 --> 00:13:51.120
Right.

00:13:53.680 --> 00:13:57.000
I lost my cap in that!

00:13:56.320 --> 00:13:57.440
We've got to get this boy on the sled.

00:13:56.560 --> 00:13:58.840
Let's get the sled here.

00:13:59.280 --> 00:14:09.440
It's back-breaking work, but they can now get the buffalo to the road and Shaun's new toy.

00:14:14.000 --> 00:14:17.960
But he hasn't read the instructions.

00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:21.880
CLANKING Not to sure what all the buttons are about.

00:14:21.680 --> 00:14:25.080
Whoa!

00:14:24.880 --> 00:14:31.400
Shaun seems to have a lovely smile on his face as he's playing with the crane.

00:14:30.440 --> 00:14:33.960
I'm not allowed to touch it.

00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:35.800
Boys with toys.

00:14:36.560 --> 00:14:40.920
It's going to be an hour's drive to get these buffalo back to base.

00:14:41.240 --> 00:14:46.320
Halfway through the journey disaster strikes.

00:14:51.680 --> 00:14:54.920
It's quite a slant and this lad's just slipped off.

00:14:55.360 --> 00:14:57.840
Let's roll that on to a sled.

00:14:56.880 --> 00:15:00.960
I'll quickly lift this one up!

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:04.960
We've just had two large buffalo slipping off the track there.

00:15:04.400 --> 00:15:07.720
Lucky it wasn't too high.

00:15:06.920 --> 00:15:11.280
And they sort of gracefully slid off as a buffalo can.

00:15:11.040 --> 00:15:15.680
After that, they're not taking any chances.

00:15:14.720 --> 00:15:21.840
We were able to get them back on, strap them in securely.

00:15:21.080 --> 00:15:25.400
It's a good result, but getting there was not good and a bit scary.

00:15:24.640 --> 00:15:27.600
We can lose animals like that as they've just had a big shock.

00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:30.160
Luckily they haven't been damaged and no guys have been damaged.

00:15:41.000 --> 00:15:53.760
On the reserve next door to Madikwe, Big John is after blue wildebeest.

00:15:52.840 --> 00:15:59.000
Once, animals could roam freely throughout South Africa but now everywhere is fenced.

00:15:58.120 --> 00:16:00.440
So their movement must be managed by man.

00:16:08.560 --> 00:16:09.640
They're coming back!

00:16:08.800 --> 00:16:11.200
Quickly, guys!

00:16:10.480 --> 00:16:11.880
Quickly!

00:16:20.320 --> 00:16:25.920
The wildebeest that we've caught are going to Madikwe.

00:16:24.960 --> 00:16:33.160
Madikwe over-caught a couple of years ago and they've got 70 lions there.

00:16:32.240 --> 00:16:38.040
So these nine are going to be lion-feed.

00:16:37.240 --> 00:16:40.120
Ja, that's nature.

00:16:39.240 --> 00:16:47.400
Wildebeest, unfortunately, aren't one of the big five.

00:16:46.680 --> 00:16:50.560
They're one of the smaller 25.

00:16:49.800 --> 00:16:52.720
So, the lions eat them.

00:16:51.920 --> 00:16:54.040
They have to be fed.

00:16:59.600 --> 00:17:06.800
As well as the wildebeest, they've caught a truck of impala.

00:17:06.000 --> 00:17:10.319
All of which are destined for Madikwe.

00:17:11.040 --> 00:17:17.599
We're opening up the doors, so if you guys just move out of the way?

00:17:22.160 --> 00:17:30.280
On Madikwe, we've been taking off the buffalo, to pay for the stocking programme.

00:17:31.120 --> 00:17:38.440
There's a huge stocking programme with 3,000 impala and 3,000 wildebeest.

00:17:37.960 --> 00:17:43.680
Basically to try and create more food for the predator population here.

00:17:42.880 --> 00:17:45.280
Doesn't want to go.

00:17:44.480 --> 00:17:47.800
"There's lions here!

00:17:46.840 --> 00:17:48.480
Please... !"

00:18:20.760 --> 00:18:26.520
It's now been a week since Emma first saw the male cheetah.

00:18:25.600 --> 00:18:30.840
She meets up with Declan to see if its leg is cancerous.

00:18:29.960 --> 00:18:32.160
Sort of pull us up quite close.

00:18:31.560 --> 00:18:34.440
I can't see him!

00:18:33.520 --> 00:18:37.040
He's exactly the same colour as the grass!

00:18:36.360 --> 00:18:40.320
There was definitely a cheetah here ten minutes ago.

00:18:39.480 --> 00:18:44.280
I can see spots.

00:18:47.600 --> 00:18:54.680
I've been sent with instructions that he's going to be named "Epekele".

00:18:53.800 --> 00:19:00.320
It's probably pronounced wrong, but it's Zulu for "broken leg".

00:18:59.720 --> 00:19:01.400
That's sweet.

00:19:00.520 --> 00:19:03.120
No, I think that's a bit of an improvement.

00:19:02.240 --> 00:19:03.560
I think so.

00:19:02.880 --> 00:19:04.200
He's putting more weight on.

00:19:04.080 --> 00:19:06.760
Before, when he was running, he'd...

00:19:05.800 --> 00:19:09.800
When he stops like that, he'd lift the leg.

00:19:09.080 --> 00:19:10.960
Now he's standing on it.

00:19:10.520 --> 00:19:12.360
He's definitely standing.

00:19:12.840 --> 00:19:23.600
If you think he's getting better and that can adapt his killing to something slower, like the guinea fowl, then he can be released.

00:19:22.680 --> 00:19:31.360
If we think he can't, we can get a nice rehab home where he doesn't have to look too hard for food.

00:19:30.440 --> 00:19:34.520
OK, I'm actually reasonably happy.

00:19:34.040 --> 00:19:35.400
We'll hold off our prognosis.

00:19:34.560 --> 00:19:36.560
We'll leave Epekele to hopefully get a bit stronger and put some more weight on that leg.

00:19:44.880 --> 00:19:49.800
The pressure's on for LJ.

00:19:49.040 --> 00:19:52.480
He's done his first darting, now it's his first boma capture.

00:19:51.840 --> 00:19:58.960
Bomas are walls of plastic that funnel herds of animals onto a lorry.

00:19:58.120 --> 00:20:06.480
The animal he's after is eland, the world's largest antelope.

00:20:06.480 --> 00:20:18.080
Usually, they run all the way through, and they're quite well behaved, but they can turn and we've got to be careful because their strong point and big weapon is the horn.

00:20:18.320 --> 00:20:22.160
He's getting very close there, hopefully he'll get into main gate.

00:20:29.800 --> 00:20:33.240
Beautiful, beautiful, beautiful, beautiful...

00:20:35.560 --> 00:20:38.760
Go, go, go!

00:20:45.160 --> 00:20:47.920
That was a really good drive.

00:20:48.960 --> 00:20:50.840
Fantastic.

00:20:49.880 --> 00:20:53.600
Do we tell him that, so he grows in confidence?

00:20:52.720 --> 00:20:56.000
No, we tell him that he missed some.

00:20:56.120 --> 00:21:04.920
The eland go onto the truck with no problems, but one of the herd is causing Emma real concern.

00:21:04.000 --> 00:21:10.720
We've got one skinny female here, and she's got a huge wound on her back.

00:21:09.800 --> 00:21:15.400
From the smell of it, it's infected.

00:21:14.560 --> 00:21:15.680
It really does stink.

00:21:14.960 --> 00:21:18.680
Let's just inject her... in her neck.

00:21:22.320 --> 00:21:24.920
OK, injection in.

00:21:24.040 --> 00:21:30.200
We're going to put her into the crush, so it's better light for me, and we can have a better look.

00:21:29.320 --> 00:21:32.480
It's too dark in the truck for me to work.

00:21:31.680 --> 00:21:34.400
It could be pretty yucky in there!

00:21:33.520 --> 00:21:36.040
Ooh!

00:21:37.360 --> 00:21:38.760
Is she stinking?

00:21:38.160 --> 00:21:41.920
I just can't handle the smell.

00:21:40.960 --> 00:21:43.520
It's my gag reflex and whatever, it just all comes up.

00:21:42.680 --> 00:21:49.520
It all started when Phoebe had her first nappy-change that I had to do, and I just...

00:21:48.560 --> 00:21:53.880
I haven't had breakfast but I think my supper might come up.

00:21:53.040 --> 00:21:58.360
They've laid eggs.

00:21:57.880 --> 00:22:00.920
Now we're going to see these tough Zulus disappearing!

00:22:01.040 --> 00:22:05.600
Don't pull the tail - I think it might come off.

00:22:04.640 --> 00:22:06.640
Oh...

00:22:06.160 --> 00:22:12.680
She is in appalling condition, and the sad fact is, I think she's just given birth and I don't think that little one is...

00:22:11.920 --> 00:22:13.400
She's had milk, as well.

00:22:12.480 --> 00:22:16.480
The best thing is to treat her.

00:22:15.720 --> 00:22:20.600
And release her, cos there's a baby out there somewhere.

00:22:19.960 --> 00:22:24.360
It's a mystery how she got this unusual wound.

00:22:23.440 --> 00:22:26.520
The one thing you can rule out is hunting.

00:22:25.640 --> 00:22:27.680
There's no hunting on the farm.

00:22:26.880 --> 00:22:28.800
Exactly.

00:22:27.960 --> 00:22:40.800
You often arrive on farms where there is hunting on it, and hunters would wound an animal and not tell the farmer about it, and so we arrive and catch a herd, and you often find wounds on the chest and abdomen.

00:22:39.880 --> 00:22:43.720
Then we'll have to try and fix that.

00:22:42.880 --> 00:22:46.680
This is a heaving mass of maggots in here.

00:22:45.720 --> 00:22:47.240
Let's get going.

00:22:46.320 --> 00:22:50.160
Our friends, the flies, have just come in and wreaked havoc.

00:22:50.960 --> 00:22:57.080
I want to get all this rotten tissue away.

00:22:56.400 --> 00:23:02.200
If this animal hadn't been caught, she would have died a really unpleasant...

00:23:01.520 --> 00:23:02.640
Argh!

00:23:02.040 --> 00:23:04.160
..Really unpleasant death.

00:23:03.320 --> 00:23:12.600
These things just keep on eating, and as long as there's oxygen, they'll keep on eating.

00:23:12.000 --> 00:23:15.680
It's amazing what nature can do to help heal this.

00:23:14.720 --> 00:23:17.080
We're helping, as well.

00:23:17.480 --> 00:23:18.640
I hope!

00:23:20.640 --> 00:23:21.720
Just ticks all over her body hair.

00:23:21.480 --> 00:23:26.560
It's literally...covered in ticks.

00:23:26.880 --> 00:23:31.240
Look in the udder here, as well.

00:23:30.360 --> 00:23:35.000
You can imagine this poor brand-new little calf having to suck these nipples... just covered in ticks.

00:23:34.240 --> 00:23:36.760
It really is horrible.

00:23:36.120 --> 00:23:40.560
We can pour some Deadline underneath.

00:23:41.000 --> 00:23:42.320
Deadline will act systemically.

00:23:41.360 --> 00:23:50.480
It's just an external drug that we put on on top of the animal, like putting flea treatment between the shoulder blades of dogs and cats.

00:23:49.560 --> 00:23:55.720
It's to try and stop the flies laying eggs for the next few days.

00:23:55.160 --> 00:23:58.400
It has to granulate and dry out on its own.

00:23:57.600 --> 00:23:59.760
And the piece de resistance - this season, we've changed our colour.

00:23:59.440 --> 00:24:04.480
We had purple spray last year.

00:24:03.680 --> 00:24:06.320
This is now a nice turquoise.

00:24:05.440 --> 00:24:06.920
A turquoise arse.

00:24:06.040 --> 00:24:09.400
A turquoise bottom.

00:24:08.720 --> 00:24:10.920
Shall we turn her around?

00:24:10.560 --> 00:24:11.600
Yeah.

00:24:11.520 --> 00:24:19.680
I'm going to give her the reversal, and it's a full reversal.

00:24:23.560 --> 00:24:27.480
Emma has done all she can.

00:24:26.600 --> 00:24:29.040
The mother now goes off to rejoin her calf somewhere in the bush.

00:24:45.040 --> 00:24:50.280
The cheetah has recovered well and can be put back on the reserve.

00:24:49.760 --> 00:24:54.920
He's been tranquilised for the journey to the release site.

00:24:56.760 --> 00:24:58.360
OK, I think I'm happy.

00:24:57.520 --> 00:25:01.720
Breathing nice, heartbeat good.

00:25:00.760 --> 00:25:01.920
OK.

00:25:01.000 --> 00:25:04.560
It was a huge bony lump.

00:25:03.640 --> 00:25:08.880
The heel fracture is called a callous.

00:25:08.040 --> 00:25:09.680
You usually get some hardened bone around the fracture site, and that's gone down big time.

00:25:10.320 --> 00:25:15.760
It was huge, and that's why, in the back of my mind, I wondered whether it was a bone tumour.

00:25:15.840 --> 00:25:22.000
I could quite happily say that that's not a bone tumour now, and he can run well on it.

00:25:21.280 --> 00:25:24.680
Condition has improved unbelievably.

00:25:24.320 --> 00:25:27.280
He was skin and bone, in really bad condition, and he's fat now.

00:25:26.560 --> 00:25:28.000
Look at his tummy!

00:25:27.400 --> 00:25:28.480
Nice big, fat tummy.

00:25:30.400 --> 00:25:35.200
I think we'll get him to his new release site as quickly as possible.

00:25:34.720 --> 00:25:40.280
The sedative should be enough for the trip.

00:25:39.480 --> 00:25:42.680
I'll keep an eye on him in the back.

00:25:41.800 --> 00:25:45.400
Hope that he doesn't wake up too soon.

00:25:46.280 --> 00:26:02.120
Declan's chosen a release site which is full of prey, full of nice, easy prey for him, so lots of guinea fowl and the slower animals, initially, and, very importantly, a low density of lions.

00:26:01.400 --> 00:26:11.640
We couldn't go to the original release site because we had a radio call that there were nine lions in the vicinity.

00:26:11.200 --> 00:26:13.840
I might have to top him up, actually.

00:26:13.040 --> 00:26:18.360
I was hoping we were going to be quite close.

00:26:17.920 --> 00:26:22.480
If I top him up, he's going to be quite sleepy again.

00:26:22.200 --> 00:26:27.160
I'm just seeing if me putting a bit of pressure on him...

00:26:29.640 --> 00:26:43.400
Oh...

00:26:43.040 --> 00:26:46.680
He didn't give me hardly any warning.

00:27:27.080 --> 00:27:34.520
He twitched and then...

00:27:33.680 --> 00:27:35.160
OK.

00:27:34.400 --> 00:27:35.920
Thank you for stopping.

00:27:35.160 --> 00:27:36.680
I appreciate that.

00:27:35.720 --> 00:27:37.440
No worries.

00:27:36.920 --> 00:27:38.000
OK.

00:27:37.160 --> 00:27:40.440
Let's try again.

00:27:39.600 --> 00:27:44.560
I'll just give him a quick MOT after that little escape plan he had there.

00:27:44.160 --> 00:27:45.320
We'll just pop in the shade here, yeah?

00:27:44.360 --> 00:27:48.800
This area here is where I first picked him up.

00:27:47.960 --> 00:27:54.960
It's nice that he's come back to... at least where he started.

00:27:54.000 --> 00:27:55.520
And now we move off...

00:27:54.640 --> 00:27:56.160
And now we go.

00:27:58.400 --> 00:27:59.640
..To stage right!

00:28:00.520 --> 00:28:06.160
He's got his first meal on a plate.

00:28:05.240 --> 00:28:09.400
After that, he'll have to fend for himself.

00:28:09.440 --> 00:28:17.880
Hey, my beautiful boy.

00:28:18.600 --> 00:28:26.200
Next week, the Ramberts catch a white rhino...

00:28:25.320 --> 00:28:31.000
It's normal, walking in the African bush, taking my rhino for a walk!

00:28:30.600 --> 00:28:34.680
I'm sitting on a now-awake rhino.

00:28:33.840 --> 00:28:36.040
..They encounter wild dogs...

00:28:35.560 --> 00:28:38.520
They're lean, mean hunting-machines.

00:28:37.640 --> 00:28:39.520
They're pulling all the insides out - revolting.

00:28:40.520 --> 00:28:44.760
..And Emma prepares to operate on a lion in the bush.

00:28:43.800 --> 00:28:44.960
What's that?

00:28:44.160 --> 00:28:45.680
Lion!

00:28:44.880 --> 00:28:46.000
Lion!

00:28:46.840 --> 00:28:55.400
Phoebes, if you see Mummy doing this to Daddy, you go call the police.
